  "live_records": {
    "q-152269": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152269",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=21-4x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}-{y}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=21-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =21-4x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x-21 & =0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-7,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-7$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,49).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(3)^{2} \\\\  & =9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,9).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=49,\\,{y}_{2}=9,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}=49-9=40.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250393/assets/8ca9ba76d66d80a0a564fbefd02342d3f63d99f6fbd907b736cdc9acee79b174.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
