    "q-152280": {
      "dom_id": "step-q152280",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The parabola $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and the line $y=2x+1$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y=2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}+1 \\\\  & =2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2{x}_{2}+1 \\\\  & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=3,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1+3=4.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250393/assets/7751473a1f9c40086a3a765487cf24f8e5430150d015cd64f1ed0c5bd62fcee4.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
