    "q-155959": {
      "dom_id": "step-q155959",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the line $y=x+2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To determine whether the line $y=x+2k$ intersects the parabola $y=2{x}^{2}-x,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2{x}^{2}-x \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-x & =x+2k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=2,\\,b=-2,$ and $c=-2k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4+16k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+16k & <0 \\\\ 16k & <-4 \\\\ k & <-\\frac{4}{16} \\\\ k & <-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<-\\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
